## Context

After #56835, the MCP server's published \`scopes_supported\` is generated from \`posthog/scopes.py\` at build time. This aligns the lists at commit time but they deploy independently:

- **Django ahead of MCP:** AS accepts a scope MCP doesn't advertise. Discovery clients miss it, hardcoded clients work fine.
- **MCP ahead of Django:** Worker advertises a scope AS rejects. Same \`invalid_scope\` bug, smaller window.

This is the deploy-race window joshsny flagged in [#56835's review](https://github.com/PostHog/posthog/pull/56835#pullrequestreview-3567005020): *\"what about scopes that are added between a django and mcp deployment?\"*

## Change

At MCP server cold start, fetch \`scopes_supported\` from the AS (\`oauth.posthog.com/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server\`) and publish only the **intersection** of (AS-grantable, bundled list).

\`\`\`ts
// services/mcp/src/index.ts (sketch)
let cachedASScopes: Set<string> | null = null;

async function getPublishableScopes(): Promise<string[]> {
  if (!cachedASScopes) {
    const res = await fetch(\`\${getAuthorizationServerUrl()}/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server\`);
    const meta = await res.json();
    cachedASScopes = new Set(meta.scopes_supported ?? []);
  }
  return OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED.filter(s => cachedASScopes!.has(s));
}

// In the protected-resource metadata handler:
scopes_supported: await getPublishableScopes(),
\`\`\`

Failure modes:
- AS unreachable at cold start: fall back to bundled list (no worse than today)
- AS scopes change at runtime: refresh on a TTL or on 401 from the AS itself

## Why

Drift becomes structurally impossible across surfaces 1↔3. The published list is provably a subset of AS-grantable, regardless of deploy order. No client behavior change because the field is still published.

## Acceptance

- MCP fetches AS scopes at cold start
- Published \`scopes_supported\` is the intersection
- Falls back to bundled list if AS unreachable
- Test that simulates an AS missing one of the bundled scopes and verifies the publication shrinks accordingly

## Tracking

Parent: #57524
Related: #56835 (#pullrequestreview comment from joshsny)
Project: https://github.com/orgs/PostHog/projects/194

### Bug Description

Hi, I have been running posthog with a pretty standard next.js reverse proxy setup, but I noticed that in the last month-ish my session recordings haven't been working. It turns out that Cloudflare seems to be blocking cloudflare-to-cloudflare connections, and I'm seeing 403 issues when requesting things e.g. `https://<my-domain>/ingest/static/posthog-recorder.js?v=1.372.1`

 I don't fully understand the issue tbh, but I haven't changed anything about this configuration since it stopped working about 12d ago, so wondering if this might be something on y'alls end. I fixed it by removing the reverse proxy for the static assets.

```
import type { NextConfig } from "next";
import { config } from "dotenv";
import path from "path";

// Load env from repo root
config({ path: path.resolve(__dirname, "../.env.local") });

const nextConfig: NextConfig = {
  async rewrites() {
    return [
      {
        source: "/ingest/decide",
        destination: "https://us.i.posthog.com/decide",
      },
      {
        source: "/ingest/static/:path*",
        destination: "https://us-assets.i.posthog.com/static/:path*",
      },
      {
        source: "/ingest/:path*",
        destination: "https://us.i.posthog.com/:path*",
      },
    ];
  },
  skipTrailingSlashRedirect: true,
};

export default nextConfig;

```

